<DocsLayout title="Haft v9" description="The published v9 source-native governance product and its deliberately smaller execution boundary.">
  <h1>Haft v9.0</h1>

  <p>
    v9 completes the governance-substrate pivot: Haft delivers versioned FPF
    source, provides bounded code and project-memory orientation, and records
    reliance-bearing project state through skills, CLI, and MCP.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Release status:</strong>
      <a href="https://github.com/m0n0x41d/haft/releases/tag/v9.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">v9.0.0</a>
      is published and is the current product. Source presence, documentation,
      or a passing local test still does not prove that a running host loaded
      that exact release.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What v9 includes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>source-native FPF concern query, exact lookup, and full-pattern inspection with provenance;</li>
    <li>12 independent host-agent skills with <code>h-reason</code> as the source-first umbrella;</li>
    <li>fused code-graph orientation and typed, reliance-gated project memory;</li>
    <li>project-profile onboarding, typed specification lifecycle, evidence currentness, and drift checks;</li>
    <li>runner-neutral WorkCommission and RuntimeRunRecord lifecycle records.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What v9 removes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><code>haft run</code> and <code>haft harness</code>;</li>
    <li>the complete Open-Sleigh source and packaged runtime;</li>
    <li>the Elixir/OTP/BEAM build, CI, installer, and archive dependency.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    There are no compatibility command stubs. Haft still governs commissioned
    work, but does not spawn an agent, manage its workspace, apply a patch, or
    publish a result. A separately operated runner may consume the typed
    lifecycle without becoming part of Haft Core.
  </p>

